WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.078
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.078 --> 00:00:12.128
SPEAKER: John Locke, the
17th-century English philosopher,

00:00:12.128 --> 00:00:18.161
argued that private property arose when
people mixed their labor with something

00:00:18.161 --> 00:00:21.792
not yet owned by anyone else.

00:00:21.792 --> 00:00:26.392
So if in the state of nature
I clear land in the wilderness

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:31.904
and grow crops there, I acquire a
right to the land and the crops.

00:00:31.904 --> 00:00:37.524
It would be wrong for someone else
to take them away without my consent.

00:00:37.524 --> 00:00:42.544
Let's see if Locke's idea helps us
think about a controversy that arises

00:00:42.555 --> 00:00:46.700
each winter in Boston after snowstorms.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.600
In Boston, many people park their
cars on the street near their homes.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.170
It takes a long time to clear a parking
place from several feet of snow.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:01:03.072
Imagine your frustration if after
spending hours digging out your car,

00:01:03.072 --> 00:01:06.062
you drive to the
grocery store and return

00:01:06.079 --> 00:01:09.753
to find another car parked
in the spot you dug out.

00:01:09.753 --> 00:01:14.230
To prevent this, people who labor
to clear snow from parking spots

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:19.140
install markers, such as
traffic cones or lawn chairs,

00:01:19.164 --> 00:01:23.254
to lay claim to them,
at least for a few days.

00:01:23.266 --> 00:01:25.650
Should this be permitted?

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:29.393
Or should anyone be free to
use a public parking spot,

00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:33.903
even if someone else has labored
long and hard to clear it?

00:01:33.903 --> 00:01:38.373
[MUSIC]

